      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Jagadeesh S. Moodera",
        "Stuart S. P. Parkin",
        "Terunobu Miyazaki"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Room-temperature tunnel magnetoresistance established a robust effect with major consequences for magnetic sensing, memory, and spintronics. It is a strong example of fundamental solid-state physics becoming useful technology.",
        "attribution": "The credited experimentalists represent important independent demonstrations and development of the room-temperature effect. The history is multi-group, so the precise balance of credit deserves careful review.",
        "maturity": "The effect has been replicated and incorporated into devices over many years; its scientific and technological importance is well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundaries between the earlier discovery of tunneling magnetoresistance and the room-temperature demonstrations may complicate attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B025",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The prediction and experimental realization of time-reversal-invariant topological insulators and the quantum spin Hall effect established a new class of quantum matter and opened a broad, lasting research field.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele supplied foundational theory, while Molenkamp's experiments established the quantum spin Hall state in HgTe quantum wells. This pairing captures the central theory-to-experiment arc.",
        "maturity": "The original effect has been experimentally confirmed and the topological framework has been applied across many material systems.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery lineage includes earlier theoretical work on topological bands, so the scope of the award would need a clear boundary."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B014",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Achim Rosch",
        "Christian Pfleiderer",
        "Yoshinori Tokura"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magnetic skyrmions revealed nanoscale topological spin textures and emergent electrodynamics in real materials. The discovery has had broad influence in magnetism and spintronics.",
        "attribution": "The credited names cover key theoretical interpretation and influential experimental identification and characterization. The discovery involved several materials and groups, so the contribution boundaries merit scrutiny.",
        "maturity": "Skyrmions have been observed in multiple settings and their basic physical properties are established, although many proposed device uses remain developmental.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The distinction between first observation, theoretical interpretation, and later demonstrations across material classes is not simple."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "A. Peter Young",
        "Bertrand I. Halperin",
        "David R. Nelson"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The theory of defect-mediated melting and the hexatic phase gave a deep account of how order is lost in two dimensions, with enduring importance across condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "The three credited theorists made complementary contributions to the framework and its development.",
        "maturity": "The theory has had decades to be tested and is supported by observations of two-dimensional melting and hexatic behavior in several systems.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Real systems can depart from ideal assumptions, and the relation between the original theory and later observations should be assessed carefully."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Claudio Castelnovo",
        "Roderich Moessner",
        "Steven T. Bramwell"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spin ice demonstrated how collective behavior in a frustrated magnet can produce excitations behaving like magnetic monopoles, a striking and influential example of emergent phenomena.",
        "attribution": "The credited work combines theoretical identification of the emergent excitations with experimental discovery and characterization of spin-ice behavior.",
        "maturity": "The spin-ice state is well established and its characteristic excitations have been studied in depth, although they are emergent quasiparticles rather than fundamental monopoles.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The popular monopole description can overstate what the experiments establish if its emergent, material-specific meaning is not made explicit."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B051",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory made a powerful framework for strongly correlated electrons practical and broadly influential, connecting local quantum dynamics to the physics of real materials.",
        "attribution": "The credited theorists were central to developing and establishing the method, though the wider intellectual lineage and community contributions require careful treatment.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been used and extended for decades, with substantial influence on the interpretation of correlated materials.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The method is an approximation whose reach depends on dimensionality and extensions; its award case rests on conceptual and methodological impact rather than one discrete experimental discovery."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B017",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Bernhard Keimer",
        "Giacomo Ghiringhelli",
        "Lucio Braicovich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "High-resolution resonant inelastic X-ray scattering made collective excitations in quantum materials experimentally accessible and has become an important probe of their dynamics.",
        "attribution": "The credited scientists represent influential instrument and measurement development, but the achievement is cumulative and involves facilities, teams, and advances beyond these individuals.",
        "maturity": "The method is established and has produced a substantial body of results, though its impact is primarily as a powerful experimental technique.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The specific advance that should define the award, and the division of credit across the broader RIXS community, are unclear."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene revealed tunable flat bands and unexpected correlated phases, creating a remarkable new platform for quantum materials research.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald predicted the flat-band mechanism, and Jarillo-Herrero's group discovered correlated insulating and superconducting behavior. The theoretical and experimental contributions are complementary.",
        "maturity": "The central observations have generated extensive follow-up work, but the mechanisms and relation among observed phases remain active areas of investigation.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field is young, with ongoing debate about superconducting mechanisms, sample dependence, and how broadly the phenomena generalize."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
